.TH @@@LISTEN@@@ 1 "27 August 1996" Linux "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
@@@listen@@@ \- monitor AX.25 traffic
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B @@@listen@@@ [-8] [-a] [-c] [-h] [-i] [-p port] [-r] [-t..] [-v]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
.B @@@Listen@@@
uses SOCK_PACKET facilities to provide a network monitor of all AX.25
traffic heard by the system. Since KISS is implicitly promiscuous no
special driver configurations are needed.
.LP
This version displays standard AX.25, PE1CHL extended AX.25, NET/ROM, Rose, ARP,
IP, ICMP, TCP and UDP. It also displays IP, TCP, ICMP, TCP and UDP
encapsulated within NET/ROM frames. The program also displays AX.25 and IP
encapsulated within an IP frame, but see RFC1326 for reasons not to do so.
.LP
.B @@@Listen@@@
makes an attempt at decoding some of the more common routing protocols. RSPF
and RIP (both \(lqnormal\(rq and G8BPQs RIP98) are traced. JNOS style
NET/ROM node polls are also displayed.
.LP
.B @@@Listen@@@
can use colors to make the output more readable. The ncurses library is used
to accomplish this.
.B Color support defaults to being disabled,
and the
.BR -c
parameter is used to enable it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 10
.BI \-8
Indicates that the terminal is capable of printing 8-bit characters. This
parameter is required for the -i parameter to work.
.TP 10
.BI \-a
Allow for the monitoring of outgoing frames as well as incoming ones.
.TP 10
.BI \-c
Enable color support.
.TP 10
.BI \-h
Dump the data portion of the packet in both hexadecimal and ASCII. The
default is to display data as ASCII only.
.TP 10
.BI \-i
Map IBM codepage 437 characters 128-158 to their ISO-Latin-1 equivalents.
This is a hack for scandinavian users. This parameter is only valid
when used with the -8 and -c parameters.
.TP 10
.BI "\-p port"
Monitor only those frames received on a particular port, by default all
AX.25 devices are monitored.
.I port
is an
.BR axports (5)
name, and need not be a kernel interface: a port that belongs to
.BR ax25netd (8)
and the
.B loop
port among them are monitored as well as any interface.  A name in no
.BR axports (5)
entry at all is refused, so a typo is not silently turned into a monitor of
everything.
.TP 10
.BI \-r
Dump the data portion in a "readable" fashion, which is more suitable
for tracing plaintext AX.25 traffic. CR-LF conversion is done.
.TP 10
.BI \-t
Don't print a timestamp on each dump line.
.TP 10
.BI -tt
Print an unformatted timestamp on each dump line.
.TP 10
.BI -ttt
Print a delta (micro-second resolution) between current and previous line
on each dump line.
.TP 10
.BI -tttt
Print a timestamp in default format proceeded by  date  on  each dump line.
.TP 10
.BI -ttttt
Print a delta  (micro-second  resolution)  between current and first
line on each dump line.
.TP 10
.BI \-v
Display the version.
.SH "WHERE THE FRAMES COME FROM"
.LP
Two mechanisms, and where the machine has both of them both are read.  Where
the kernel serves AX.25 there is a packet socket, and this program needs
nothing said about it.  Where
.B libax25
serves AX.25 from userspace instead \-\- see
.BR axsock (7)
\- there is a monitor stream, and what fills it depends on the backend:
.TP 8
AGWPE
works.  The server's raw monitor is switched on with the first packet socket
and off again with the last, and every frame it hears arrives.
.B ax25netd (8)
(or
.BR direwolf )
fills the monitor itself; it has to be reachable for the socket to carry
anything.
.TP 8
WAMPES
works for UI frames, through the same
.B ax25netd (8)
monitor channel.  WAMPES has no monitor stream of its own, so the libax25
side pushes the UI frames it carries \-\- what it receives from the node and
what it sends to it \-\- into
.BR ax25netd (8),
where a
.B @@@listen@@@
program picks them up like any AGWPE frame.  It picks them up on the loop
port, so a frame of a WAMPES node is reported with
.B loop
as its port: that is the AGWPE port it crossed, not the node's own name.  The
digipeaters of a frame
received from the node carry the
.B *
mark, as on the air.  Connected sessions are not mirrored; a WAMPES node
traces on its own console instead, and that is where to look.
.LP
All of them, in one run.  Where the kernel serves AX.25 there is a packet
socket; where
.B libax25
serves it from userspace there is a
.BR ax25netd (8)
monitor stream.  A machine that has both has both, and both are read: the kernel
socket for the ports the kernel owns, the monitor for the rest \-
including the WAMPES traffic, which is mirrored into the
.BR ax25netd (8)
monitor channel because there is no packet socket for a node to put frames on.
A run that used one or the other showed half the band without saying so, which
for the program whose whole job is to show the band is the worst kind of wrong.
An
.BR ax25netd (8)
that is not up costs nothing here: the kernel side keeps working, and the monitor
side starts receiving by itself as soon as the server is reachable again \-
there is nothing to restart.  With no kernel AX.25 stack at all there is still
the
.BR ax25netd (8)
monitor, and with neither there is nothing to monitor, which is said on standard
error rather than shown as a run that prints nothing.
.IP
Which of the two are in use is reported under
.BR AXSOCK_DEBUG .
See
.BR axsock (7).
.LP
If one of the two ends while the other is running \-
the
.BR ax25netd (8)
side, normally \-
the loss is said on standard error and the rest of the run carries on.  Only
when the last source is gone does
.B @@@listen@@@
stop, with a non-zero exit status.  An
.BR ax25netd (8)
that is being restarted comes back on its own, without restarting
.BR @@@listen@@@ .
.SH FILES
@@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/axports
